      "type": "thinking",
      "thinking": "Let me analyze this fragment carefully.\n\nThis is an OIML Certificate:\n- Number: R49/2013-A-NL1-20.02 revision 3\n- Scheme: A\n- Project number: 3846781\n- Page total: 6 (Page 1 of 6)\n- Member State: The Netherlands\n- Date issued: 30 July 2024\n- Issuing authority: NMi Certin B.V., OIML Issuing Authority NL1\n- Person responsible: M.Ph.D. Schmidt\n- Address: NMi Certin B.V., Thijsseweg 11, 2629 JA Delft, The Netherlands\n- Phone: +31 88 636 2332\n- Email: certin@nmi.nl\n- Website: www.nmi.nl\n\nApplicant and Manufacturer: KROHNE Altometer, Kerkeplaat 12, 3313 LC Dordrecht, The Netherlands\n\nCertified type: electromagnetic water meter, Type: WATERFLUX 3070\n\nRecommendation: R 49-1 (2013) - canonical id: \"R49\" or \"R49-1\"? The canonical format per examples: \"R60\" not \"OIML R 60\". For R49-1, I'd say id \"R49-1\" since that's the part designation. Edition: 2013? The schema says edition (int). Hmm, R49-1 (2013) — the year is the edition. Actually the recommendation R 49-1:2013, edition would be... The example for R60 says edition (int). For \"R 49-1 (2013)\", I'll put edition: 2013. Hmm, but edition typically means edition number like 2. The year 2013 for R49-1 is actually edition 2013. I'll set edition to 2013? Actually let me think. OIML R 49-1:2013 — the year indicates the edition year. The schema example: `id (canonical, e.g. \"R60\" not \"OIML R 60\"), edition (int), amendment, scheme, accuracy_classes[]`. I'll use edition: 2013. Accuracy classes: [1, 2].\n\nTest reports:\n- R49-1/2006-NL1-09.01 dated 16 September 2009, 41 pages, 14 annexes — role: type_evaluation_report\n- R49-1/2006-NL1-10.01 dated 9 April 2010, 40 pages, 3 annexes\n- R49-1/2006-NL1-11.01 dated 2 May 2011, 40 pages, 4 annexes\n- R49-1/2006-NL1-12.01 dated 28 March 2012, 40 pages, 3 annexes\n- NMi-13200194-01 dated 18 July 2013, 2 pages, 1 annex\n- NMi-13200159-01 dated 18 March 2015, 6 pages, 1 annex\n- NMi-15200645-01 dated 30 March 2016, 21 pages, 4 annexes\n- NMi-2224507-01 dated 30 December 2020, 27 pages, 4 annexes\n\nRole: these are reports associated with the type evaluation. The first set are probably Type Evaluation Reports; the later NMi ones are additional test reports. I'll mark the first (R49-1/2006-NL1-09.01) as type_evaluation_report and the others as test_report. Hmm, actually all could be TERs (revisions/additions). Hard to say. The certificate says \"conformity was established by the results of tests and examinations provided in the associated reports\". The Documentation folder is no. T10201-11 — that's a documentation_file.\n\nPages field: schema says pages (probably int). But there are \"41 pages and 14 annexes\". I'll put pages: 41, and mention annexes? The schema doesn't have annexes. Maybe include pages as int only. Or I could add \"annexes\" as extra — schema says don't fabricate, but adding fields beyond schema... Better to stay in schema. I could note pages: 41. Maybe include annexes in role description? No. I'll keep pages as int. Alternatively, I can keep it simple.\n\nRevision history: revision \"3\", date 30 July 2024. Changes: not stated. So revision_history: [{\"revision\": \"3\", \"date\": \"2024-07-30\"}].\n\nCertified type:\n- category: \"Water meter\"\n- type_designations: [\"WATERFLUX 3070\"]\n- module_designation: null (not mentioned) — omit or null.\n- description: \"An electromagnetic water meter\"\n\nModel family: The certificate covers a family of instruments with meter sizes DN25 through DN600. Table 2 gives flow rates per meter size and accuracy class.\n\nNow the big task: extract Table 2 (pages 3-4) as matrix_tables and model_level characteristics.\n\nTable structure: Meter size (DN), Accuracy class, Q1, Q2, Q3, Q4 (m³/h), Ratio Q3/Q1.\n\nSome meter sizes have multiple rows because multiple accuracy class variants (class 2 rows and class 1 rows), and for a given class there are two rows with different flow rate sets (e.g., DN25 class 2 has rows: 0,025/0,04/10/12,5/400 and 0,04/0,064/16/20/400). These two rows differ in Q3/Q1? Both have ratio 400... wait let me check.\n\nDN25 class 2:\n- Row 1: Q1=0.025, Q2=0.04, Q3=10, Q4=12.5, ratio 400\n- Row 2: Q1=0.04, Q2=0.064, Q3=16, Q4=20, ratio 400 (rowspan 2 for ratio)\n\nHmm both have ratio 400. So two variants for class 2 DN25. These likely correspond to different Q3 values (10 vs 16 m³/h). So variants per DN with different Q3.\n\nDN40 class 2:\n- Row 1: 0.0625, 0.1, 25, 31.25, ratio 400\n- Row 2: 0.1, 0.16, 40, 50, ratio 400\n\nDN50 class 2:\n- Row 1: 0.1, 0.16, 40, 50, ratio 400\n- Row 2: 0.1575, 0.252, 63, 78.75, ratio 400\n\nDN65:\n- class 2 rows: (0.1575, 0.252, 63, 78.75, 400), (0.25, 0.4, 100, 125, 400)\n- class 1 row: (0.4, 0.64, 100, 125, ratio 250)\n\nDN80:\n- class 2 rows: (0.25, 0.4, 100, 125, 400), (0.4, 0.64, 160, 200, 400)\n- class 1 rows: (0.625, 1, 100, 125, 160), (0.64, 1.024, 160, 200, 250)\n\nDN100:\n- class 2 rows: (0.4, 0.64, 160, 200, 400), (0.625, 1, 250, 312.5, 400)\n- class 1 rows: (1, 1.6, 160, 200, 160), (1, 1.6, 250, 312.5, 250)\n\nDN125:\n- class 2 rows: (0.625, 1, 250, 312.5, 400), (1, 1.6, 400, 500, 400)\n- class 1 rows: (1.5625, 2.5, 250, 312.5, 160), (1.6, 2.56, 400, 500, 250)\n\nDN150:\n- class 2 rows: (1, 1.6, 400, 500, 400), (1.575, 2.52, 630, 787.5, 400)\n- class 1 rows: (2.5, 4, 400, 500, 160), (2.52, 4.032, 630, 787.5, 250)\n\nDN200:\n- class 2 row: (1.575, 2.52, 630, 787.5, 400)\n- class 1 row: (3.9375, 6.3, 630, 787.5, 160)\n\nDN250:\n- class 2 row: (2.5, 4, 1000, 1250, 400)\n- class 1 row: (6.25, 10, 1000, 1250, 160)\n\nDN300:\n- class 2 row: (4, 6.4, 1600, 2000, 400)\n- class 1 row: (10, 16, 1600, 2000, 160)\n\nDN350: class 1 or 2: (15.625, 25, 2500, 3125, 160)\nDN400: class 1 or 2: (25, 40, 4000, 5000, 160)\nDN450: class 1 or 2: (25, 40, 4000, 5000, 160)\nDN500: class 1 or 2: (39.375, 63, 6300, 7875, 160)\nDN600: class 1 or 2: (63, 100.8, 6300, 7875, 100)\n\nSo the model axis is really meter size + accuracy class + Q3 variant. Total models: let me count rows: DN25:2, DN40:2, DN50:2, DN65:3, DN80:4, DN100:4, DN125:4, DN150:4, DN200:2, DN250:2, DN300:2, DN350:1, DN400:1, DN450:1, DN500:1, DN600:1 = 2+2+2+3+4+4+4+4+2+2+2+1+1+1+1+1 = 36 rows.\n\nModel IDs: I'll define models like \"DN25 class 2 Q3=10\", or simply per row \"DN25-a\". Better: model_id = \"DN25/2/10\" — hmm. Perhaps use \"DN25, class 2, Q3=10 m³/h\". Let me use model_id like \"DN25-2-10\" with label \"DN25, accuracy class 2, Q3=10 m³/h\"? The schema: models[]: {model_id, label, attributes{}}. \n\nSimpler approach: model_id = \"DN25 class2 Q3=10\". Hmm, verbose but fine. Maybe cleaner: \"DN25_cl2_Q3=10\". I'll use something readable: \"DN25 class 2 Q3=10\".\n\nActually, maybe I should keep model ids compact: \"DN25-2-10\" etc. But readability matters. I'll go with \"DN25 class 2 Q3=10\".\n\nFor DN350-DN600 with \"1 or 2\", model_id could be \"DN350 class 1 or 2 Q3=2500\".\n\nGiven 36 models, listing model_level characteristics for q1, q2, q3, q4, ratio per model = 5 attributes × 36 values. Plus matrix_tables with 36 rows. That's a lot of JSON but appropriate per the schema and the R137 example which populates both matrix_tables and model_level.\n\nLet me construct the matrix table:\n\ncolumns: [\"meter_size\", \"accuracy_class\", \"q1\", \"q2\", \"q3\", \"q4\", \"ratio_q3_q1\"]\ncolumn_units: [null, null, \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", null]\n\nrows: 36 objects with model keys.\n\nLet me enumerate all rows carefully with values (converting decimal commas):\n\n1. DN25, class 2: Q1=0.025, Q2=0.04, Q3=10, Q4=12.5, ratio=400\n2. DN25, class 2: Q1=0.04, Q2=0.064, Q3=16, Q4=20, ratio=400\n3. DN40, class 2: 0.0625, 0.1, 25, 31.25, 400\n4. DN40, class 2: 0.1, 0.16, 40, 50, 400\n5. DN50, class 2: 0.1, 0.16, 40, 50, 400\n6. DN50, class 2: 0.1575, 0.252, 63, 78.75, 400\n7. DN65, class 2: 0.1575, 0.252, 63, 78.75, 400\n8. DN65, class 2: 0.25, 0.4, 100, 125, 400\n9. DN65, class 1: 0.4, 0.64, 100, 125, 250\n10. DN80, class 2: 0.25, 0.4, 100, 125, 400\n11. DN80, class 2: 0.4, 0.64, 160, 200, 400\n12. DN80, class 1: 0.625, 1, 100, 125, 160\n13. DN80, class 1: 0.64, 1.024, 160, 200, 250\n14. DN100, class 2: 0.4, 0.64, 160, 200, 400\n15. DN100, class 2: 0.625, 1, 250, 312.5, 400\n16. DN100, class 1: 1, 1.6, 160, 200, 160\n17. DN100, class 1: 1, 1.6, 250, 312.5, 250\n18. DN125, class 2: 0.625, 1, 250, 312.5, 400\n19. DN125, class 2: 1, 1.6, 400, 500, 400\n20. DN125, class 1: 1.5625, 2.5, 250, 312.5, 160\n21. DN125, class 1: 1.6, 2.56, 400, 500, 250\n22. DN150, class 2: 1, 1.6, 400, 500, 400\n23. DN150, class 2: 1.575, 2.52, 630, 787.5, 400\n24. DN150, class 1: 2.5, 4, 400, 500, 160\n25. DN150, class 1: 2.52, 4.032, 630, 787.5, 250\n26. DN200, class 2: 1.575, 2.52, 630, 787.5, 400\n27. DN200, class 1: 3.9375, 6.3, 630, 787.5, 160\n28. DN250, class 2: 2.5, 4, 1000, 1250, 400\n29. DN250, class 1: 6.25, 10, 1000, 1250, 160\n30. DN300, class 2: 4, 6.4, 1600, 2000, 400\n31. DN300, class 1: 10, 16, 1600, 2000, 160\n32. DN350, class 1 or 2: 15.625, 25, 2500, 3125, 160\n33. DN400, class 1 or 2: 25, 40, 4000, 5000, 160\n34. DN450, class 1 or 2: 25, 40, 4000, 5000, 160\n35. DN500, class 1 or 2: 39.375, 63, 6300, 7875, 160\n36. DN600, class 1 or 2: 63, 100.8, 6300, 7875, 100\n\nModel IDs: I'll do e.g. \"DN25 class 2 Q3=10\", \"DN25 class 2 Q3=16\", \"DN40 class 2 Q3=25\", \"DN40 class 2 Q3=40\", \"DN50 class 2 Q3=40\", \"DN50 class 2 Q3=63\", \"DN65 class 2 Q3=63\", \"DN65 class 2 Q3=100\", \"DN65 class 1 Q3=100\", \"DN80 class 2 Q3=100\", \"DN80 class 2 Q3=160\", \"DN80 class 1 Q3=100\", \"DN80 class 1 Q3=160\", \"DN100 class 2 Q3=160\", \"DN100 class 2 Q3=250\", \"DN100 class 1 Q3=160\", \"DN100 class 1 Q3=250\", \"DN125 class 2 Q3=250\", \"DN125 class 2 Q3=400\", \"DN125 class 1 Q3=250\", \"DN125 class 1 Q3=400\", \"DN150 class 2 Q3=400\", \"DN150 class 2 Q3=630\", \"DN150 class 1 Q3=400\", \"DN150 class 1 Q3=630\", \"DN200 class 2 Q3=630\", \"DN200 class 1 Q3=630\", \"DN250 class 2 Q3=1000\", \"DN250 class 1 Q3=1000\", \"DN300 class 2 Q3=1600\", \"DN300 class 1 Q3=1600\", \"DN350 class 1 or 2 Q3=2500\", \"DN400 class 1 or 2 Q3=4000\", \"DN450 class 1 or 2 Q3=4000\", \"DN500 class 1 or 2 Q3=6300\", \"DN600 class 1 or 2 Q3=6300\".\n\nThat's verbose. 36 models each appearing in 5 model_level attributes × 36 values — huge. Maybe I should limit model_level to key attributes and keep the full table in matrix_tables. The rules say extract matrix as matrix_tables AND populate model_family.models and characteristics.model_level (R137 example shows qmax, qt, qmin model_level). For this big table, model_level entries for q1, q2, q3, q4, ratio_q3_q1, accuracy_class would be 6 attributes × 36 values = 216 value objects. Plus matrix table with 36 rows. It's a lot but doable. However, the output length constraints... The instructions emphasize matrix detection as most important, so I should do it thoroughly. But maybe to keep it manageable, I'll include model_level for q1, q2, q3, q4 and ratio_q3_q1, and accuracy_class as a model-level attribute too. Hmm.\n\nActually, maybe a compromise: include the matrix_table fully, and model_level entries for the flow rates and ratio. That mirrors the example. Accuracy class per model is in matrix rows and in model attributes; including it also as model_level seems redundant but the R137 example didn't include class either (no class column there).\n\nI think I'll include model_level for: accuracy_class (value string like \"2\", \"1\", \"1 or 2\"), q1, q2, q3, q4, ratio_q3_q1. That's 6 × 36 = 216 entries. That's quite big but fine.\n\nHmm, to reduce size maybe drop accuracy_class from model_level since it's in matrix and model attributes. The example model_level had only qmax, qt, qmin. I'll include accuracy_class anyway? Let me decide: include it — it varies by model, so per rule 4 varying attributes go to model_level. But matrix_tables also has it. The example puts them both in matrix AND model_level. OK include.\n\nActually wait — values in model_level follow the structured form? Rule 2 says every characteristic value MUST be an object with value/unit_symbol. In the R137 example, model_level values are `{\"model\": \"G1.6\", \"value\": 2.5}` — no unit_symbol inside each value; unit_symbol is at attribute level. So the shape within model_level values: {model, value} and unit at attribute level. Footnote markers would be per value. OK, follow the example: {\"model\": ..., \"value\": ...}.\n\nFor type_level characteristics (Table 1):\n\n- measuring_principle: \"Electromagnetic\"\n- accuracy_class: \"1 and 2\" — hmm, this is type-level. value: \"1 and 2\"? Or since accuracy classes vary by model... certificate-level says accuracy class 1 and 2. I'll put type_level accuracy_class: {value: \"1 and 2\"}? Actually recommendation.accuracy_classes: [1, 2] covers it. And model-level class in matrix. I might still include type_level accuracy_class with value \"1 and 2\". Hmm, rule 8: don't fabricate but it is present. I'll include as {value: \"1 and 2\"}. Or omit since per-model. I'll include; harmless.\n\n- environmental_class: varies by software version: \"M2 and O (installed outdoors) for software version 5.0.1_ or higher; B (installed in a building) for software version 4.3.1_ or lower\". This is a config axis (software version). I could model as config_level: attribute \"environmental_class\", axis \"software_version\". Values: [{condition: \"software version 5.0.1_ or higher\", value: \"M2 / O (installed outdoors)\"}, {condition: \"software version 4.3.1_ or lower\", value: \"M2 / B (installed in a building)\"}].\n\nWait — the table says Environmental class = \"M2<br><b>O (installed outdoors)</b> for software version 5.0.1_ or higher<br><b>B (installed in a building)</b> for software version 4.3.1_ or lower\". So M2 is the environmental class for all, with O or B additional. Per R49/EN61010-ish, M2 is environmental class and O/B... Actually in OIML D11 environmental classes: B (condensed), O (outdoor)? Hmm, in MID WELMEC guidance, environmental classes M2 and O (open air) or B (building). So value: M2 with subclasses. I'll represent:\n\nconfig_level: attribute \"environmental_class\", axis \"installation_software_version\":\n- condition \"software version ≥ 5.0.1_\", value \"O (installed outdoors)\" — as string with base \"M2\"? I'll record environmental_class: \"M2\" at type_level, and config-level for outdoor/building variant? Simpler: type_level environmental_class: \"M2 (O installed outdoors for software ≥5.0.1_; B installed in a building for software ≤4.3.1_)\". That's messy.\n\nBetter structured approach:\n- type_level: {environmental_class: \"M2\"}\n- config_level: attribute \"environmental_class_installation_variant\"? Hmm.\n\nI'll do:\n- type_level: environmental_class: {value: \"M2\"}\n- config_level: [{attribute: \"installation_class\", axis: \"software_version\", values: [{condition: \"software version 5.0.1_ or higher\", value: \"O (installed outdoors)\"}, {condition: \"software version 4.3.1_ or lower\", value: \"B (installed in a building)\"}]}]\n\n- temperature_range_ambient: config_level with axis software_version:\n  - condition \"software version 5.0.1_ or higher\": {min: -25, max: 55}, unit °C\n  - condition \"software version 4.3.1_ or lower\": {min: -10, max: 55}, unit °C\n\nPer rule, config_level values: {condition, value, unit_symbol}. The R5 example shape: `characteristics.config_level` with `axis` and values `{condition, value}`. I'll include unit_symbol per value since unit may be per value: {condition, value: {min, max}, unit_symbol: \"°C\"}.\n\n- water_temperature_class: \"T50 (+0.1 °C / +50 °C)\". Canonical: water_temperature_class: {value: \"T50\"}. And maybe water_temperature_range as config? I'll add type_level water_temperature_class: {value: \"T50\"} and water_temperature_range: {value: {min: 0.1, max: 50}, unit_symbol: \"°C\"}. Note \"+0,1 °C / +50 °C\" → min 0.1, max 50. Yes decimal comma conversions.\n\n- maximum_admissible_pressure: \"1,6 MPa (16 bar)\" → value: 1.6, unit_symbol: \"MPa\". Could add alternative unit \"16 bar\" — I'll add a separate characteristic maximum_admissible_pressure_alt: {value: 16, unit_symbol: \"bar\"}? Or include in same? Schema wants single value. I'll add one characteristic \"maximum_admissible_pressure\" value 1.6 MPa and another \"maximum_admissible_pressure_alt_bar\" hmm, that's fabricated naming. Maybe just record 1.6 MPa. Or record both via a note. I'll do maximum_admissible_pressure {value: 1.6, unit_symbol: \"MPa\"} and maximum_admissible_pressure_bar {value: 16, unit_symbol: \"bar\"}? Eh. I'll include just the primary with value 1.6 MPa, and mention 16 bar in... Actually I can add \"maximum_admissible_pressure\" with value 1.6 unit MPa; the \"(16 bar)\" is secondary. Keep it. Hmm but \"don't fabricate\" — including the bar alternative is not fabrication. I'll add characteristic `maximum_admissible_pressure_equivalent` value 16 unit bar. Fine.\n\n- orientation: \"All positions (Horizontal, vertical or diagonal)\" → orientation: {value: \"All positions (horizontal, vertical or diagonal)\"}.\n\n- flow_profile_sensitivity_class: \"U0 and D0 (0 x DN upstream and 0 x DN downstream)\" → flow_profile_sensitivity_class: {value: \"U0 and D0\"}, and flow_profile_installation: {value: \"0 x DN upstream and 0 x DN downstream\"}? I'll record flow_profile_sensitivity_class: \"U0 and D0\" and maybe straight_pipe_requirements: \"0 x DN upstream / 0 x DN downstream\". Keep it simple: one characteristic with value \"U0 and D0 (0 x DN upstream and 0 x DN downstream)\". Strings allowed.\n\n- reverse_flow: {value: \"The sensor is designed to measure reverse flow\"} → simpler: reverse_flow: {value: \"sensor designed to measure reverse flow\"}? Don't paraphrase too much; keep original: \"The sensor is designed to measure reverse flow\".\n\n- pressure_loss_class: \"Δp 63 (0,63 bar)\" → pressure_loss_class: {value: \"Δp 63\"}, pressure_loss: {value: 0.63, unit_symbol: \"bar\"}. Δp is a symbol, not LaTeX, fine. Maybe avoid \"Δ\" for JSON safety? It's fine in JSON (UTF-8). I'll keep \"Δp 63\" but maybe canonical label pressure_loss_class value \"63\" class. I'll do pressure_loss_class: {value: \"Δp 63\"} and pressure_loss_max: {value: 0.63, unit_symbol: \"bar\"}.\n\n- power_supply: multiple alternatives:\n  - 3.6 V D-cell Lithium replaceable battery\n  - Flexpower (software ≥5.0.1): 3.6 V D-cell lithium non-replaceable battery; 9…30 VDC; 100…230 VAC / 50–60 Hz ±2%\n\nRepresent as:\n- config_level power_supply axis? Or components? These are power supply options, alternatives OR. I'll use:\n  - type_level: power_supply_type: {value: \"3.6 V D-cell Lithium replaceable battery\"}? Hmm.\n\nBetter: \n- battery_voltage: {value: 3.6, unit_symbol: \"V\"}\n- battery_type: {value: \"D-cell Lithium\"}\n- battery_replaceable: config? (replaceable for standard; non-replaceable for Flexpower) → config_level axis \"software_version\": replaceable for ≤4.3.1_, non-replaceable for ≥5.0.1_. Hmm.\n\nI'll do:\n- type_level: power_supply: {value: \"3.6 V D-cell Lithium replaceable battery or Flexpower\"}\n- config_level: attribute \"power_supply\", axis \"software_version_and_option\":\n  - {condition: \"software version 4.3.1_ or lower\", value: \"3.6 V D-cell Lithium replaceable battery\"}\n  - {condition: \"software version 5.0.1_ or higher (Flexpower)\", value: \"3.6 V D-cell Lithium battery (non-replaceable) or external supply\"}\n- type_level numeric: power_supply_voltage_dc: {value: {min: 9, max: 30}, unit_symbol: \"V\"} (9…30 VDC)\n- power_supply_voltage_ac: {value: {min: 100, max: 230}, unit_symbol: \"V\"}\n- power_supply_frequency: {value: {min: 50, max: 60}, unit_symbol: \"Hz\"} with note ±2% → maybe include \"power_supply_tolerance\": {value: 2, unit_symbol: \"%\"}? The \"+/-2 %\" applies to the whole. I'll add footnote-like remark in description? Keep: include power_supply_frequency with value {min:50,max:60} and add type_level power_supply_tolerance_percent {value: 2, unit_symbol: \"%\"}? Hmm, that could be misinterpreted. I'll include it as part of a separate characteristic \"power_supply_voltage_frequency_tolerance\": {value: \"±2%\"}. Simpler: skip ±2% or record as its own char: power_supply_tolerance: {value: 2, unit_symbol: \"%\"}. I'll include it.\n\nRule 3 example explicitly shows splitting \"110-240V AC 50/60Hz\" into power_supply_voltage, power_supply_frequency, power_supply_type. Here we have two options (battery or external). I'll record:\n\ntype_level:\n- battery_voltage: {value: 3.6, unit_symbol: \"V\"}\n- battery_type: {value: \"D-cell Lithium\"}\n- power_supply_voltage_dc: {value: {min: 9, max: 30}, unit_symbol: \"V\"} (DC)\n- power_supply_type_dc: hmm, \"9 … 30 VDC\" → value range 9–30 V, type DC. I'll name \"power_supply_voltage_dc\" — the type embedded in label.\n- power_supply_voltage_ac: {value: {min: 100, max: 230}, unit_symbol: \"V\"}\n- power_supply_frequency: {value: {min: 50, max: 60}, unit_symbol: \"Hz\"}\n- battery_replaceable: config_level axis software_version.\n\nSoftware identification table: software versions with CRC checksums. This is a mapping software version → CRC. Where to put? Could be matrix_tables: name \"Software versions and CRC checksums\", columns [\"software_version\", \"crc_checksum\"], rows: many versions. The first cell lists versions 4.0.4_, 4.0.10_, 4.0.11_, 4.0.12_, 4.2.2_, 4.2.4_, 4.2.5_, 4.2.6_, 4.3.0_, 4.3.1_ with CRC \"Not applicable\"; then 5.0.1_ → 4Cb5; 5.0.2_ → 71d5; 5.0.3_ → CFF7; 5.0.5_ → dCAb; 5.1.0_ → Ab62.\n\nI'll emit matrix_tables entry for this. Also the note about Field Current 16 mA → footnotes? It's a \"Note:\" not a numbered footnote. I can include in software matrix description or as footnotes entry? Footnotes schema expects markers. I'll put it in the matrix description and also maybe in certified_type description? I'll add to matrix description.\n\nModel family: family_name: \"WATERFLUX 3070\"? The type is WATERFLUX 3070 and the cert covers a family. family_name: \"WA